{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "373",
    "case_summary": "A 69-year-old woman with a history of hypertension and stage IA estrogen receptor–positive and progesterone receptor–positive and ERBB/neu receptor–negative invasive ductal carcinoma of the left breast presented with blurry vision in both eyes. The patient had started receiving 1 mg of anastrozole daily 6 months prior to her presentation. She was not using any other notable medications, denied any recent changes to her regimen, and had never previously used tamoxifen.\n\nBest-corrected visual acuity was 20/40 OD and 20/25 OS. Pupils were equal, round, and reactive without an afferent pupillary defect. Intraocular pressures were normal. Anterior segment examination was only notable for mild cataracts in both eyes. Fundus examination was notable for parafoveal shimmering golden crystalline deposits in the right and left eyes.\n\n- Imaging studies:\n  1. Fundus examination:\n    Image Description: Parafoveal shimmering golden crystalline deposits in both eyes. The crystalline deposits did not display autofluorescence, suggesting that they were not lipofuscin granules.\n\n  2. Spectral-domain optical coherence tomography:\n    Image Description: Crystalline deposits located at the level of the outer plexiform layers with some extension into the inner nuclear area subfoveally.",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Bilateral Visual Disturbance with Crystalline Deposits",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
